const e={"Dominican Republic":[{emoji:"🤙",headline:"Dominicans greet everyone in the room",body:'Walking into a room without greeting each person individually is considered rude. Even in a busy colmado, a quick "buenas" to the whole room is the minimum. Leaving without saying goodbye to each person is equally noticed.',tip:"Start every interaction with a greeting — skipping it makes you seem cold or arrogant, not efficient."},{emoji:"🎵",headline:"Bachata was born in the Dominican Republic",body:"Bachata originated in the DR in the 1960s and was considered low-class music for decades before going global. Today it's a source of enormous national pride. You'll hear it in colmados, cars, and parties at all hours.",tip:"Mentioning bachata or merengue positively will immediately warm up any Dominican conversation."},{emoji:"🍲",headline:"Sancocho is the national soul food",body:"Sancocho is a slow-cooked stew with multiple meats, root vegetables, and herbs. It's made for celebrations, Sundays, and hangovers. Being invited to someone's sancocho is a genuine act of hospitality.",tip:"If someone invites you to sancocho, say yes — it's one of the highest forms of Dominican hospitality."},{emoji:"🏪",headline:"The colmado is the center of neighborhood life",body:`The colmado is far more than a corner store — it's where people gather, buy on credit ("fiado"), gossip, and listen to music. The owner often knows every family in the block by name.`,tip:"Treat the colmado owner with warmth and take your time — rushing through is considered impersonal."},{emoji:"⚾",headline:"Baseball is the national religion",body:"The Dominican Republic has produced more Major League Baseball players per capita than any other country. Names like Pedro Martínez, David Ortiz, and Albert Pujols are household legends. Baseball conversations are a fast track to Dominican hearts.",tip:"Knowing even one Dominican MLB player by name will earn you instant credibility in any conversation."},{emoji:"🗣️",headline:"Dominican Spanish drops letters constantly",body:'Dominicans are known for speaking fast and dropping the "s" at the end of words — "¿cómo está?" becomes "¿cómo etá?". The "r" often sounds like "l" — "puerta" sounds like "puelta". This is normal, not sloppy.',tip:"Don't panic if you can't understand Dominicans at first — even fluent Spanish speakers need time to adjust to the rhythm."},{emoji:"💃",headline:"Merengue is the official music of the DR",body:"Merengue was declared the official music and dance of the Dominican Republic. It's fast, rhythmic, and deeply tied to national identity. The accordion, güira, and tambora are its signature instruments.",tip:"If music starts at a gathering, people are expected to dance — standing still on the sidelines can seem standoffish."},{emoji:"🌴",headline:'"Quisqueya" is what Dominicans call their homeland',body:`The indigenous Taíno name for Hispaniola was Quisqueya, meaning "mother of all lands." Dominicans still use this name with pride — you'll see it on jerseys, in songs, and in everyday speech.`,tip:'Using "Quisqueya" when referring to the DR shows genuine respect and cultural awareness.'}],"Puerto Rico":[{emoji:"☕",headline:"Puerto Rican coffee is among the world's finest",body:"Coffee from the mountainous regions of Yauco, Jayuya, and Lares has been considered among the best in the world since the 19th century. A cortadito or café con leche from a local cafetería is a daily ritual.",tip:"Complimenting Puerto Rican coffee is a genuine conversation starter — locals are intensely proud of it."},{emoji:"🇺🇸",headline:"Puerto Ricans are US citizens but not US state residents",body:"Puerto Rico is a US territory — its residents are American citizens from birth but cannot vote in presidential elections and have no voting representation in Congress. The question of statehood vs. commonwealth vs. independence is always politically alive.",tip:'Asking about "el status" (the political status debate) shows genuine interest — just be ready for a passionate response.'},{emoji:"🎉",headline:"Las Fiestas Patronales happen in every town",body:"Every municipality in Puerto Rico holds annual patron saint festivals with live music, food stalls, carnival rides, and dancing. They last up to two weeks and are central to local identity.",tip:"If you visit during a fiesta patronal, go — it's the most authentic window into local life you'll find."},{emoji:"🍽️",headline:"Mofongo is the unofficial national dish",body:"Mofongo is fried plantain mashed with garlic, olive oil, and pork rinds, shaped into a ball and often stuffed with seafood or meat. It's everywhere — from roadside stalls to upscale restaurants.",tip:"Ordering mofongo shows you've done your homework. Asking what it's stuffed with starts a good conversation."},{emoji:"⚾",headline:"Baseball and boxing are Puerto Rico's sporting hearts",body:"Puerto Rico has produced legendary baseball players (Roberto Clemente, Carlos Beltrán) and world champion boxers (Miguel Cotto, Wilfredo Gómez). Roberto Clemente in particular is treated almost as a saint — his humanitarian legacy is enormous.",tip:"Roberto Clemente is a deeply emotional topic — knowing his story and showing respect for it will move people."},{emoji:"🎵",headline:"Reggaeton was largely born in Puerto Rico",body:"Though it drew from Jamaican dancehall and Panamanian influences, reggaeton developed its modern form in Puerto Rico in the 1990s. Artists like Daddy Yankee, Bad Bunny, and Don Omar brought it to the world.",tip:"Bad Bunny is currently the most-streamed artist in the world — bringing him up is an instant connection with younger Puerto Ricans."},{emoji:"🌿",headline:"Spanglish is a completely natural part of daily speech",body:`Code-switching between Spanish and English mid-sentence is completely normal in Puerto Rico — it's not confusion, it's cultural fluency. "Voy al mall" or "está on sale" are unremarkable in daily conversation.`,tip:"Don't be surprised by English words in Spanish sentences — just follow the flow naturally."},{emoji:"🏠",headline:"La familia extends far beyond the nuclear family",body:`In Puerto Rico, "family" includes cousins, godparents (compadres), and neighbors who've been close for decades. Sunday family gatherings are large, multigenerational, and central to Puerto Rican identity.`,tip:"Asking about someone's family — even extended relatives — is a sign of genuine interest, not intrusion."}],Mexico:[{emoji:"🌽",headline:"Corn is sacred — literally and culturally",body:"The Mayan Popol Vuh says humans were created from corn. Mexico is the genetic origin of maize and has over 60 native varieties. Tortillas, tamales, atole, and pozole all begin with corn — it's the foundation of Mexican civilization.",tip:"Never underestimate the tortilla — asking about regional corn traditions opens deep, proud conversations."},{emoji:"💀",headline:"Día de los Muertos is a celebration, not a mourning",body:"The Day of the Dead (November 1-2) is a joyful tradition rooted in indigenous Aztec practices merged with Catholic All Saints' Day. Families build ofrendas (altars) with photos, food, and marigolds to welcome back the spirits of the dead.",tip:`Treating Día de los Muertos as a "Mexican Halloween" will offend. It's a profound cultural and spiritual tradition.`},{emoji:"🌶️",headline:"Mexican food varies enormously by region",body:'Oaxacan cuisine features mole negro and tlayudas. Yucatán has cochinita pibil and sopa de lima. The north is beef and flour tortilla country. What tourists know as "Mexican food" is really just one tiny slice.',tip:"Asking someone where they're from and what people eat there is one of the best conversations you can have in Mexico."},{emoji:"⚽",headline:"Football (soccer) is a near-religious passion",body:"Club América vs. Guadalajara (El Clásico Nacional) is one of the most passionate rivalries in world football. The Mexican national team's defeats and victories provoke genuine national emotion. The 1986 World Cup goal by Hugo Sánchez is still celebrated.",tip:"Ask which club team someone supports before choosing a side — the rivalry between clubs is fierce."},{emoji:"👨‍👩‍👧‍👦",headline:"Family hierarchy is deeply respected",body:"The abuela (grandmother) is often the most powerful figure in a Mexican household — socially if not officially. Respect for elders (respeto) is not just polite but a genuine cultural value that shapes how decisions are made.",tip:"Showing genuine respect to older family members, especially grandmothers, will earn you enormous goodwill."},{emoji:"🤝",headline:'"Palanca" — having the right connection — is everything',body:'Palanca literally means "lever" and refers to knowing someone who can help you navigate systems, get jobs, or solve problems. In Mexico, who you know often matters more than what you know in professional and bureaucratic situations.',tip:"Building personal relationships before asking for anything is always the right approach in Mexico."},{emoji:"🎶",headline:"Mariachi is a UNESCO cultural heritage",body:"Mariachi music originated in Jalisco in the 19th century. A traditional mariachi group includes violins, trumpets, guitarrón, and vihuela. Hiring a mariachi to serenade someone — especially for birthdays or proposals — is still very much alive.",tip:'"Cielito Lindo" and "Ay, Jalisco No Te Rajes" are songs every Mexican knows — recognizing them earns instant warmth.'},{emoji:"🕐",headline:'"Ahorita" does not mean "right now"',body:`Ahorita is one of Mexico's most flexible words — it can mean "in a moment," "later," "sometime today," or even "never." It's not rude or dishonest; it's a social lubricant that softens hard commitments.`,tip:`If you need something at a specific time, say the exact time — don't rely on "ahorita" as a firm commitment.`}],French:[{emoji:"🥖",headline:"Fresh bread every day is not optional",body:"French people buy bread fresh daily — sometimes twice. The baguette tradition (a law protected since 1993) means the bread must be made on-site. Walking to the boulangerie in the morning is a genuine daily ritual, not a quaint tradition.",tip:"Bringing a fresh baguette to someone's home is always appropriate — it shows you understand how things work."},{emoji:"🤝",headline:"Bonjour is non-negotiable",body:'Starting any interaction — entering a shop, asking a question, getting on an elevator — without saying "Bonjour" first is considered genuinely rude. Not cold. Rude. The word opens every door in France.',tip:'Say "Bonjour" before anything else, every time, without exception. It costs nothing and changes everything.'},{emoji:"🍽️",headline:"French meals follow a strict structure",body:"A proper French meal has entrée (starter), plat (main), and dessert or cheese. Eating your main course and then immediately leaving is unusual. The meal is a social event with distinct phases — rushing it is considered strange.",tip:"Never ask for the bill (l'addition) while others are still eating — it signals you want to leave and that's everyone's cue."},{emoji:"💬",headline:"Intellectual debate is a social sport",body:"Arguing — rigorously, passionately, and at length — is considered a sign of intelligence and engagement in France. Cafés, dinner tables, and workplaces are all arenas for debate. Agreeing with everything is actually considered slightly suspect.",tip:"Having a clear opinion and defending it respectfully earns more respect than being agreeable and vague."},{emoji:"🧀",headline:"France has over 1,000 varieties of cheese",body:'Charles de Gaulle once asked, "How can you govern a country that has 246 types of cheese?" The real number is higher. Cheese is served as its own course before dessert, not with crackers at a party.',tip:"Asking someone their favorite cheese and why is a conversation that can last the entire meal."},{emoji:"👗",headline:"French style is about restraint, not excess",body:'The French concept of "chic" is about looking effortlessly put-together, not overdressed. Less is more — one statement piece, neutral colors, and excellent quality basics. Overdressing for casual situations is as wrong as underdressing.',tip:"Dressing well but simply in France signals you understand the culture — it opens social doors."},{emoji:"🎭",headline:"May 68 still shapes French political culture",body:"The student and worker uprising of May 1968 permanently changed French society — labor laws, university reforms, and social freedoms all shifted. The French still reference it as a touchstone for protest and collective action.",tip:"Understanding that the French have a genuine tradition of productive protest helps explain many conversations about politics."},{emoji:"🏖️",headline:"August is when France shuts down",body:"Most French people take their annual vacation in August. Paris empties — restaurants and shops close, the city feels different. The French right to five weeks of paid vacation is taken seriously and considered sacred.",tip:"Don't schedule important meetings or expect quick business responses in August — it's vacation season across the country."}],Parisian:[{emoji:"☕",headline:"The zinc bar is where Parisians actually drink coffee",body:"Standing at the zinc (metal) bar in a café is cheaper, faster, and far more local than sitting at a table. The same espresso costs two to three times more if you sit on the terrasse. Most Parisians drink their coffee in under two minutes.",tip:`Stand at the bar, say "un café s'il vous plaît," drink it in 90 seconds. That's how Parisians do it.`},{emoji:"🚇",headline:"The Paris Métro is the city's circulatory system",body:"The Paris Métro has 16 lines, 302 stations, and runs from 5:30am to 1am (2am on weekends). It's one of the densest metro networks in the world. Parisians navigate it automatically — looking lost with a map marks you immediately.",tip:"Download the RATP app before arriving — knowing your line and direction before getting on signals local confidence."},{emoji:"🗼",headline:"Parisians rarely go to the Eiffel Tower",body:`The Eiffel Tower, the Louvre, and Notre-Dame are for tourists and school trips. Most Parisians haven't been inside many of these landmarks for decades. The "real" Paris is in the arrondissements, the marchés, and the neighborhood cafés.`,tip:"Ask a Parisian about their neighborhood (arrondissement) — that's where they actually live and what they're proud of."},{emoji:"📚",headline:"Paris was home to the greatest literary expatriate scene in history",body:"In the 1920s, Hemingway, Fitzgerald, Gertrude Stein, James Joyce, and Ezra Pound all lived and worked in Paris. Shakespeare and Company bookshop is still there. Parisian intellectual culture absorbs this heritage with genuine pride.",tip:"Mentioning a French author or philosopher you've read (Camus, Sartre, Beauvoir) earns immediate intellectual respect."},{emoji:"🥐",headline:"A good croissant is judged by its layers and butter",body:"A proper croissant parisien should be curved, deeply golden, flaky in layers, and taste distinctly of good butter. Straight croissants are usually made with margarine. Parisians can tell the difference instantly and judge a boulangerie accordingly.",tip:"Asking a local which boulangerie they use for croissants is a question that will get you a passionate, specific answer."},{emoji:"🌆",headline:"Each arrondissement has its own personality",body:"Paris's 20 arrondissements spiral out from the center. The Marais (3rd/4th) is historic and LGBTQ+ friendly. Montmartre (18th) is bohemian and hilly. Canal Saint-Martin (10th) is young and creative. Locals identify strongly with their neighborhood.",tip:'Asking "tu es de quel arrondissement?" is a fast way into a genuine conversation about Parisian life.'}],"Southern French":[{emoji:"🌅",headline:"Life in the South moves at a different pace",body:"The French Midi (South) is famous for its slower rhythm — long lunches, afternoon breaks, markets that wind down by noon. This is not laziness; it's a conscious relationship with time and pleasure that Southerners are proud of.",tip:"Don't schedule anything important between 12-3pm in smaller Southern French towns — you will find closed doors."},{emoji:"🌿",headline:"Pastis is the unofficial drink of the South",body:"Pastis is an anise-flavored aperitif that turns cloudy white when you add water. Drinking it slowly on a sunny terrace in the afternoon is a quintessential Southern French ritual. Ricard and Pernod are the main brands.",tip:"Ordering a pastis in the South signals you know the culture — it will often start a conversation."},{emoji:"🎭",headline:"The Camargue, Provence, and the Languedoc are distinct regions",body:"The South of France is not one culture. The Camargue has flamingos and wild horses. Provence has lavender fields and Roman ruins. The Languedoc has its own language (Occitan) and distinct culinary traditions.",tip:"Asking someone specifically which part of the South they're from shows genuine awareness — Southerners are proud of these distinctions."},{emoji:"🐟",headline:"Bouillabaisse is serious business in Marseille",body:"The famous Marseille fish stew has a protected recipe — the real thing must contain at least four specific Mediterranean fish. Restaurants in Marseille that serve tourist bouillabaisse with random fish are considered fraudulent.",tip:'Never claim to have had "the best bouillabaisse" to a Marseillais without being prepared for a detailed debate.'},{emoji:"☀️",headline:"The Mistral wind shapes everything in Provence",body:"The Mistral is a powerful cold wind that blows down the Rhône Valley into Provence, sometimes for days. It clears the air and creates the famous clear light of Provence that drew Van Gogh and Cézanne. It also frays nerves.",tip:"Mentioning the Mistral to someone from Provence will always get a strong reaction — everyone has a story about it."},{emoji:"🗣️",headline:"The Southern French accent is immediately recognizable — and mocked",body:'The Southern French accent (accent du Midi) is characterized by pronouncing the silent "e" at the end of words and a distinctive nasal quality. Parisians often mock it; Southerners are fiercely proud of it.',tip:"Never imitate or comment negatively on the Southern accent — they've heard the jokes."},{emoji:"🏟️",headline:"Rugby is religion in the Southwest — not football",body:"While most of France follows football, the Southwest (Toulouse, Bordeaux, Biarritz) is rugby country. Rugby clubs like Stade Toulousain are as culturally significant as the biggest football clubs.",tip:"In the Southwest, asking about rugby rather than football marks you as someone who knows the region."},{emoji:"🛒",headline:"The morning market is the social heart of Southern French towns",body:"Southern French markets are not just for shopping — they're where the community gathers and begins the day. Vendors know their regulars. Markets in Aix-en-Provence, Arles, and Uzès are legendary.",tip:"Go to a Southern market without a list — buy what's freshest, and the vendors will tell you what to do with it."}],Italian:[{emoji:"🍕",headline:"Pizza in Italy bears no resemblance to international pizza",body:"A Neapolitan pizza has a thin, charred, slightly chewy base with minimal toppings. Roman pizza (pizza al taglio) is sold by weight in rectangular slices. Thick crusts, stuffed crusts, and multiple toppings are considered vulgar.",tip:'"Pepperoni" is not an Italian word — peperoni (one p) means bell peppers. If you want spicy sausage, ask for salame piccante.'},{emoji:"☕",headline:"Cappuccino after 11am is a tourist tell",body:"Italians drink cappuccino only at breakfast — milky coffee after a meal is considered bad for digestion. After lunch or dinner, it's a short espresso (un caffè). Ordering a cappuccino after noon marks you immediately as foreign.",tip:`After a meal, just say "un caffè" — you'll get a perfect espresso and look like you know what you're doing.`},{emoji:"👨‍👩‍👧‍👦",headline:"La famiglia is the true center of Italian life",body:"Italian family ties are intense and multigenerational. Many Italians live near their parents or grandparents well into adulthood — not from lack of independence but from genuine cultural preference. Sunday lunch with the whole family is non-negotiable in many households.",tip:"Asking about someone's family — parents, grandparents, siblings — shows respect for what Italians actually value most."},{emoji:"🤌",headline:"Italian hand gestures have specific meanings",body:`The famous pinched fingers gesture (ma che vuoi?) means "what do you want?" or "what are you talking about?" Other gestures convey "perfect," "I don't care," and "let's go eat." Gestures are not random — they're a parallel language.`,tip:"Watching Italian gestures and asking what they mean is a conversation that will make any Italian laugh and teach you immediately."},{emoji:"⚽",headline:"Calcio is a religion with its own saints",body:"Italian football (Serie A) has some of the world's most passionate fan bases. The north-south divide is present in football too — Juventus (Turin), Inter and AC Milan vs. Napoli and Roma. Roberto Baggio, Paolo Maldini, and Francesco Totti are legends.",tip:"Asking which club team someone supports is always safe — just don't express a conflicting allegiance immediately."},{emoji:"🎨",headline:"Italy has the most UNESCO World Heritage Sites in the world",body:"Italy has more UNESCO World Heritage sites than any other country — over 60 of them. The Colosseum, Pompeii, the Cinque Terre, the Amalfi Coast, Venice, Florence's historic center — each is a living cultural artifact that Italians engage with daily.",tip:"Asking an Italian about what's special about their specific region always unlocks pride-filled conversation."},{emoji:"🧾",headline:"The scontrino (receipt) is legally required at restaurants",body:"In Italy, tax authorities require restaurants to issue a receipt (scontrino fiscale). You can technically be fined if caught leaving a restaurant without one. It's not bureaucracy — it's a tax compliance system that's taken seriously.",tip:"Always take your receipt when leaving an Italian restaurant — you might technically need it outside the door."},{emoji:"⏰",headline:"Italian time is its own concept",body:'"Un attimo" (one moment) can mean anywhere from 30 seconds to 3 days. Being 15-20 minutes late to social events is normal in most of Italy. Dinner starts at 8pm at the earliest, often 9pm. The schedule is approximate; the pleasure is the point.',tip:"Don't be the first person to arrive at an Italian dinner — arriving exactly on time reads as eager or foreign."},{emoji:"🏙️",headline:"Italy is really many countries in one",body:"Northern Italy (Milan, Turin) is Germanic in work culture, fashion-forward, and punctuality-minded. Southern Italy (Naples, Palermo) is warm, slower-paced, and community-driven. Rome is its own chaotic, ancient, theatrical world. These aren't stereotypes — Italians themselves make these distinctions.",tip:"Always establish where in Italy someone is from before assuming anything about their personality or customs."},{emoji:"👗",headline:'"La bella figura" shapes all Italian behavior',body:'"La bella figura" — literally "the beautiful figure" — is the Italian principle of always presenting yourself well: dressed appropriately, composed, speaking correctly, behaving with dignity. Its opposite, "la brutta figura" (making a bad impression), is to be avoided at all costs.',tip:"Dressing well in Italy is not vanity — it's a form of respect for the people around you. Underdressing reads as rudeness."},{emoji:"☕",headline:"Espresso at the bar is a daily ritual",body:"Italians drink espresso standing at the bar — sitting costs more. A coffee at the counter takes 90 seconds. It's a daily punctuation mark, not a beverage to linger over. The barista often knows your order before you say it.",tip:'Ordering a "large coffee" or a Starbucks-style drink will mark you as foreign instantly — order un caffè and drink it standing.'},{emoji:"🎭",headline:"Opera and classical music are living culture, not museums",body:"La Scala in Milan, the Arena di Verona, the San Carlo in Naples — these are not heritage sites for tourists. They are living institutions where Italians go regularly and hold strong opinions. Booing a bad performance at La Scala is a long tradition.",tip:"Expressing genuine interest in opera — even just asking which composer someone prefers — opens a passionate conversation with culturally-minded Italians."}],Neapolitan:[{emoji:"🍕",headline:"Neapolitan pizza is protected by law",body:"The Associazione Verace Pizza Napoletana (AVPN) sets strict rules for authentic Neapolitan pizza — San Marzano tomatoes, 00 flour, cooked in a wood-fired oven at 485°C, eaten within minutes of leaving the oven. It's a UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage.",tip:`Telling a Neapolitan their pizza is "like" something you had elsewhere is not a compliment — it's an insult.`},{emoji:"🤌",headline:"Touch is a language in Naples",body:"Neapolitans are physically warm — touching an arm, a shoulder, or a back during conversation is completely normal. Personal space is smaller here than in northern Italy or northern Europe. It's warmth, not intrusion.",tip:"Don't stiffen when someone touches your arm during conversation — it's a sign of genuine connection."},{emoji:"☕",headline:"Neapolitan coffee is the gold standard",body:`Naples is generally considered to produce Italy's best espresso — a longer extraction, more body, and slightly more bitter than the rest of Italy. Caffè sospeso (a "suspended coffee" paid forward for someone who can't afford one) is a Neapolitan tradition centuries old.`,tip:"Participating in caffè sospeso — paying for an extra coffee for a stranger — is a small act that locals will deeply appreciate."},{emoji:"⚽",headline:"Diego Maradona is a god in Naples",body:"When Maradona led Napoli to their first Serie A titles in 1987 and 1990, he became a deity to Neapolitans. Murals of him cover entire buildings. When he died in 2020, Naples went into genuine mourning. The stadium is named after him.",tip:"Talking about Maradona with reverence in Naples is an instant emotional connection — it runs extremely deep."},{emoji:"🎭",headline:"Pulcinella is Naples's eternal folk character",body:"Pulcinella — a masked, hunchbacked character from Commedia dell'Arte — is the symbol of the Neapolitan spirit: cunning, irreverent, and resilient under pressure. You'll see his image everywhere in the city.",tip:"Understanding Pulcinella as a symbol of Neapolitan resilience — not just a puppet — shows genuine cultural depth."},{emoji:"🌋",headline:"Vesuvius is not a backdrop — it's a presence",body:"Mount Vesuvius looms over Naples and the entire bay. Neapolitans live with the knowledge that it's an active volcano — and have for centuries. The attitude is a kind of magnificent fatalism: life is short, so live it fully.",tip:`The Neapolitan expression "vedi Napoli e poi muori" (see Naples and then die) captures this spirit perfectly — it's not dark, it's a celebration.`},{emoji:"🎶",headline:"Neapolitan song is Italy's most famous musical tradition",body:`"O Sole Mio," "Funiculì Funiculà," and "Santa Lucia" are all Neapolitan songs, not generically Italian. Naples had a conservatory before Milan. The city's musical tradition spans centuries and is an enormous source of local pride.`,tip:'Recognizing a classic Neapolitan song — not just calling it "Italian" — earns real appreciation.'}],Portuguese:[{emoji:"🎵",headline:"Fado is the soul of Portugal",body:`Fado (from "fatum," Latin for fate) is a melancholic, deeply emotional music tradition born in Lisbon's Alfama district in the 19th century. It's built around "saudade" — a longing for something lost. Amália Rodrigues is the undisputed queen of fado.`,tip:"Listening quietly and respectfully during fado is essential — talking during a performance is a serious breach."},{emoji:"💭",headline:'"Saudade" is untranslatable but essential',body:"Saudade is a Portuguese word with no direct equivalent — it describes a deep emotional longing for something or someone absent, mixed with the pleasure of that longing. It's a genuine cultural worldview, not just a poetic concept.",tip:"When a Portuguese person mentions saudade, take it seriously — they're sharing something emotionally significant."},{emoji:"🐟",headline:"Bacalhau (salted cod) has 365 ways to be cooked",body:"Portugal has a saying that there's a different bacalhau recipe for every day of the year. Salted cod was the fish of Portuguese sailors and explorers — it preserved well on long voyages. It's now considered the national dish in many of its forms.",tip:"Asking which bacalhau preparation is someone's favorite is a question that will start a long and enthusiastic conversation."},{emoji:"⚽",headline:"Football is the national obsession",body:'The "Big Three" — Benfica, Sporting CP, and FC Porto — divide Portuguese loyalty absolutely. Cristiano Ronaldo is a national hero of near-mythological status. Portuguese football conversations are passionate and detailed.',tip:'Ask which of the "Big Three" someone supports before mentioning any team — the rivalry is intense.'},{emoji:"🌍",headline:"Portugal had the longest-lasting colonial empire",body:"From the 15th century until 1999 (Macau's handover to China), Portugal maintained overseas territories. This history — celebrated and contested — is still present in architecture, language, and national identity. Portuguese is now spoken by 250 million people worldwide.",tip:"The colonial history is a sensitive topic that requires genuine knowledge and care — not a casual conversation starter."},{emoji:"🚋",headline:"Lisbon's tram 28 is an institution",body:"Tram 28 winds through Lisbon's oldest and hilliest neighborhoods — Alfama, Mouraria, Grão Vasco. It's not just transport; it's a moving piece of the city's architecture. The yellow trams (eléctricos) are a genuine Lisbon symbol.",tip:"Riding tram 28 slowly, getting off to explore — not as a tourist sprint — is how Lisboetas actually experience their city."},{emoji:"🏄",headline:"Portugal has some of the world's best waves",body:"Nazaré holds the record for the world's largest surfed wave (over 26 meters). The Algarve, Ericeira, and Peniche are world-class surf destinations. Surfing culture has grown enormously and is now a major part of Portuguese coastal identity.",tip:"If you're near the coast, surf culture is a genuine conversation topic — it's not just a tourist activity here."}],Brazilian:[{emoji:"⚽",headline:"Football is a national religion with five World Cups",body:'Brazil has won the FIFA World Cup five times — more than any other nation. Pelé is universally considered the greatest footballer of all time. The loss to Uruguay in the 1950 final (the "Maracanazo") is still mourned as a national trauma.',tip:"Asking about someone's favorite Brazilian footballer will always spark a passionate, detailed response."},{emoji:"🎉",headline:"Carnival is four days of organized national joy",body:"Brazilian Carnival (the four days before Lent) is the world's largest festival. Rio's samba school parades are elaborate competitive performances. Salvador's carnival is a massive street party. São Paulo's is the largest LGBT+ event in the world.",tip:"Knowing that Carnival varies enormously by city shows cultural depth — Rio's version is not the only one."},{emoji:"🌳",headline:"The Amazon is 60% of Brazil's territory",body:"The Amazon rainforest covers 5.5 million km² — roughly the size of western Europe. It contains 10% of all species on Earth and 20% of the world's fresh water. Brazilians have enormously varied views on development vs. conservation.",tip:"Deforestation and indigenous rights are politically charged topics — listen carefully before weighing in."},{emoji:"🍖",headline:"Churrasco is a social ritual, not just a meal",body:"Brazilian barbecue (churrasco) is a multi-hour social event centered on slowly grilled meats. The churrasqueiro (grill master) is a position of genuine respect. Sunday churrasco with family is a cornerstone of Brazilian life.",tip:"Arriving at a churrasco too early means you're helping set up — arriving on time means you're ready to eat."},{emoji:"🎵",headline:"Brazil invented bossa nova and gave it to the world",body:'Bossa nova emerged in Rio de Janeiro in the late 1950s — João Gilberto, Tom Jobim, and Vinicius de Moraes created a genre that influenced jazz worldwide. "The Girl from Ipanema" is one of the most recorded songs in history.',tip:"Knowing bossa nova and samba as distinct traditions — not the same thing — shows genuine musical awareness."},{emoji:"🌊",headline:"The beach is a social institution in Rio",body:"Ipanema and Copacabana beaches in Rio are not just sunbathing spots — they're outdoor living rooms. Different sections are informally designated by neighborhood, sexuality, and social group. The beach has its own etiquette, vendors, and social hierarchy.",tip:"In Rio, the beach you go to and the section you use communicates something about you — locals read this immediately."},{emoji:"🤝",headline:"Jeitinho brasileiro — the Brazilian way of finding a way",body:"Jeitinho is the Brazilian art of solving problems creatively, bending rules resourcefully, and navigating systems with charm. It's simultaneously celebrated as Brazilian ingenuity and criticized as a workaround culture. Every Brazilian has a strong opinion on it.",tip:'Understanding jeitinho explains a lot about Brazilian social dynamics — when someone says "a gente dá um jeito," they mean it.'},{emoji:"🇧🇷",headline:"Brazil is not a Spanish-speaking country",body:"Portuguese is the language of Brazil — a legacy of colonial history. Brazilian Portuguese sounds quite different from European Portuguese. Brazilians are often surprised when foreigners assume they speak Spanish.",tip:"Never assume a Brazilian speaks Spanish — asking in English first is more respectful than assuming."},{emoji:"💃",headline:"Brazil has more dance styles than almost anywhere on Earth",body:"Samba, forró, axé, baião, pagode, funk carioca, lambada — each region of Brazil has its own dance identity. Forró from the Northeast is intimate and joyful. Funk from Rio is raw and urban. Knowing the difference impresses Brazilians immediately.",tip:"Asking where in Brazil someone is from before discussing music and dance will save you from making regional assumptions."},{emoji:"🤗",headline:"Brazilians are physically warm in ways that surprise outsiders",body:"Kisses on the cheek are standard greetings even with new acquaintances. Personal space is smaller. Touching someone's arm during conversation is normal warmth, not invasion. Brazilians find northern European reserve confusing and sometimes rude.",tip:"Lean into physical warmth — stiffening during a cheek-kiss greeting reads as cold or unfriendly to Brazilians."}],"Quebec French":[{emoji:"🗣️",headline:"Quebec French preserved sounds France abandoned",body:'Quebec French kept features of 17th-century French that France itself dropped — including the affrication of t and d before "i" and "u" sounds (tu sounds like "tsu", dire like "dzire"). This makes it sound dramatically different from Parisian French.',tip:"Don't assume your Parisian-French ear will transfer — give yourself time to adjust to the affrication and vowels."},{emoji:"❄️",headline:"Winter is a core part of the identity",body:"Winter defines Quebec culture — many areas average over 300cm of snow a year. Complaining about winter, then surviving it with visible pride, is a genuine social ritual rather than real grievance.",tip:"Joining in a good-natured winter complaint is a fast way to bond — it is shared ritual, not negativity."},{emoji:"🍟",headline:"Poutine is rural Quebec's gift to the world",body:"Poutine — fries, fresh cheese curds, and brown gravy — originated in rural Quebec in the late 1950s. The curds must be fresh enough to squeak. Long dismissed as working-class food, it became a national symbol.",tip:'Calling poutine "Canadian" rather than Quebecois can mildly annoy locals — it is theirs first.'},{emoji:"🕊️",headline:"The Quiet Revolution remade Quebec in a decade",body:"The Quiet Revolution of the 1960s transformed Quebec from a conservative, Church-dominated society into a modern secular state in under a decade — one of the fastest social transformations in North American history.",tip:"Understanding how recently and deliberately Quebec secularized helps explain its strong feelings about language and identity."},{emoji:"🗳️",headline:"The 1995 referendum still shapes everything",body:"The 1995 Quebec sovereignty referendum failed by about 50,000 votes — 50.58% No to 49.42% Yes. The emotional weight of that near-miss has never fully dissipated and still shapes Quebec politics and identity.",tip:"Sovereignty is a live, sensitive subject — listen more than you opine until you know the room."},{emoji:"🏒",headline:"Les Canadiens are a cultural institution",body:"The Montreal Canadiens are more than a hockey team. The 1955 Richard Riot — sparked when star Maurice Richard was suspended — became a flashpoint for French-Canadian identity against English domination.",tip:"Knowing even a little Canadiens history earns real credibility in casual conversation."},{emoji:"🎭",headline:"Montreal punches far above its weight in the arts",body:"Quebec has one of North America's most vibrant francophone arts scenes — from Cirque du Soleil to the Montreal International Jazz Festival, among the largest jazz festivals in the world by number of concerts.",tip:"Asking about local festivals is an easy, warm conversation opener in Montreal."},{emoji:"💬",headline:"Joual is reclaimed working-class pride",body:"Joual is the working-class Montreal dialect — anglicisms, contractions, distinctive pronunciation. Once stigmatized, it was reclaimed in the 1960s as a marker of authentic Quebecois identity.",tip:"Don't mock joual features — they carry real cultural pride for many speakers."}]},d={Colombian:"Colombia",Sicily:"Sicilian","European Portuguese":"Portugal",Mexican:"Mexico","Mexican Spanish":"Mexico","Castilian Spanish":"Castilian",Spain:"Castilian","Haitian Creole":"Haiti",Creole:"Haiti",Brazilian:"Brazil","Brazilian Portuguese":"Brazil",Paris:"Parisian",Naples:"Neapolitan",Italian:"Italy","Standard Italian":"Italy",Quebec:"Quebec French",Quebecois:"Quebec French","French Canadian":"Quebec French"};function h(n,t){var a;const o=[n,t,d[n],d[t]];for(const i of o)if(i&&((a=e[i])!=null&&a.length))return{key:i,facts:e[i]};return null}function c(n,t,o=3){const a=h(n,t);if(!a)return[];const{facts:i}=a,s=Math.max(1,Math.floor(i.length/o)),r=[];for(let l=0;l<o&&l*s<i.length;l++)r.push(i[l*s]);return r}function u(n,t){const o=h(n,t);if(!o)return null;const{facts:a}=o,i=new Date().toDateString()+(n||t||"");let s=0;for(const r of i)s=s*31+r.charCodeAt(0)&4294967295;return a[Math.abs(s)%a.length]}e.Cuba=[{emoji:"🎺",headline:"Cuba gave the world son, mambo, and salsa",body:"Cuban music is one of the most influential in history. Son cubano, developed in the 19th century, became the foundation for salsa, mambo, and cha-cha-chá. Havana's music scene produced legends like Celia Cruz and Compay Segundo.",tip:"Showing appreciation for Cuban music — especially the classic trova and son styles — earns immediate warmth from Cubans."},{emoji:"🚗",headline:"Classic American cars are a living museum",body:`Cuba's streets are filled with 1950s American cars kept running through decades of improvisation. These "almendrones" are both taxis and symbols of Cuban ingenuity. Mechanics here can fix anything with whatever they have.`,tip:"Complimenting someone's car is a great conversation starter — Cubans are proud of keeping these machines alive."},{emoji:"🍬",headline:'"Resolver" is the Cuban philosophy of life',body:`"Resolver" (to resolve) describes the Cuban art of finding creative solutions to scarcity and bureaucracy. It's not just a word — it's a survival skill and source of national pride. If a Cuban says "lo voy a resolver," trust them.`,tip:`Using "resolver" shows you understand Cuban reality — it's one of the most important words in the culture.`},{emoji:"🌆",headline:"Havana has two economies running side by side",body:"Cuba operates with two currencies and two economic realities — the state economy and the informal one. Cubans navigate both daily with remarkable skill. The informal economy (buying, selling, trading) is how most things actually get done.",tip:"Cubans appreciate directness about economic realities — pretending they don't exist comes across as naive."},{emoji:"⚾",headline:"Baseball is Cuba's national obsession",body:"Cubans have played baseball since the 1860s and produced some of the greatest players in history. The Cuban national team was legendary for decades. You'll find baseball being played in every neighborhood, park, and empty lot.",tip:"Baseball knowledge opens doors in Cuba like almost nothing else — mentioning a Cuban baseball legend earns instant respect."},{emoji:"🤝",headline:"Cubans are extraordinarily warm to strangers",body:"Despite economic hardship, Cuban hospitality is legendary. Offering what little they have to guests is a point of pride. Being invited into a Cuban home is a genuine honor — don't refuse food or coffee if it's offered.",tip:"Always accept what's offered in a Cuban home — refusing feels like a rejection of the person, not just the food."},{emoji:"💬",headline:"Cuban Spanish is fast, musical, and drops syllables",body:'Cubans speak with a distinctive sing-song rhythm and drop the "s" at the end of words constantly. "¿Qué tú dices?" is standard Cuban syntax — using "tú" before the verb is a signature Cuban construction not found elsewhere.',tip:'The "¿Qué tú dices?" construction marks you as knowing Cuban Spanish specifically — use it and locals will notice.'},{emoji:"🏥",headline:"Cuba has one of the world's best healthcare systems",body:"Despite being a small island nation, Cuba has a doctor-to-patient ratio that rivals wealthy nations. Cuban doctors have served in over 60 countries. Healthcare is a source of enormous national pride and frequent conversation.",tip:"Acknowledging Cuban medical achievements is one of the safest and most appreciated compliments you can give."}];e.Mexico=[{emoji:"🌮",headline:"Mexican cuisine is a UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage",body:"In 2010, UNESCO recognized traditional Mexican cuisine as intangible cultural heritage — one of the first cuisines ever recognized this way. The diversity across regions is staggering: Oaxacan mole, Yucatecan cochinita, Veracruz seafood are entirely different worlds.",tip:"Asking a Mexican where they're from often unlocks a passionate conversation about regional food — every state has something to defend."},{emoji:"👨‍👩‍👧‍👦",headline:"Family is the foundation of Mexican society",body:"In Mexico, family loyalty runs deep. Sunday family lunches (la comida familiar) are sacred. Grandparents, aunts, uncles, and cousins are involved in daily life in ways that would surprise many outsiders. Decisions are often made with family input.",tip:"Asking about someone's family is always appropriate and shows genuine interest — Mexicans are proud to talk about their families."},{emoji:"🎭",headline:"Día de Muertos is a celebration, not a mourning",body:"The Day of the Dead (November 1-2) is not a sad occasion — it's a joyful reunion with deceased loved ones. Families build altars (ofrendas), visit cemeteries, and celebrate the lives of those who passed. It predates Spanish colonization by thousands of years.",tip:"Treating Día de Muertos as a death celebration rather than a life celebration misses the entire point — Mexicans will gently correct you."},{emoji:"🤠",headline:"Regional identity is fiercely important",body:"Mexicans identify strongly with their state — being Tapatío (from Guadalajara), Regiomontano (from Monterrey), or Chilango (from Mexico City) shapes personality, slang, food, and worldview. These identities can cause friendly rivalries.",tip:"Learning someone's regional origin is key to understanding their slang, humor, and food references."},{emoji:"🎵",headline:"Mariachi is the sound of Mexican celebration",body:"Mariachi bands are hired for birthdays, serenatas (romantic serenades), quinceañeras, and funerals. A proper mariachi performance is an emotional event. The tradition originated in Jalisco and is now a UNESCO heritage.",tip:"Expressing love for mariachi music — especially specific songs — creates immediate connection with older Mexicans."},{emoji:"☕",headline:"Café de olla is the original Mexican coffee",body:"Traditional Mexican coffee is brewed in a clay pot with cinnamon and piloncillo (raw sugar). It's warming, earthy, and utterly different from espresso culture. Finding it in a market or home is a genuine cultural experience.",tip:"Asking for café de olla instead of regular coffee in a traditional setting shows cultural knowledge that locals appreciate."},{emoji:"🏛️",headline:"Mexico has the most UNESCO World Heritage Sites in the Americas",body:"Mexico's 30-plus UNESCO sites include Chichen Itza, Teotihuacan, Oaxaca's historic center, and the colonial cities of Guanajuato and Puebla. Pre-Hispanic civilization here was among the most sophisticated in human history.",tip:"Showing interest in Mexico's pre-Hispanic history — not just the Aztecs but the Maya, Zapotec, and others — is deeply appreciated."},{emoji:"💪",headline:'"No hay mal que por bien no venga" — Mexican resilience',body:"Mexicans have a remarkable capacity to find humor and resilience in hardship. Dark humor (albures), irreverence toward authority, and finding joy in daily life are cultural survival strategies refined over centuries.",tip:"Laughing with Mexicans at life's absurdities — not at Mexico — is one of the fastest ways to build genuine connection."}];e.Colombia=[{emoji:"☕",headline:"Colombian coffee is a national identity",body:`Colombia is the world's largest producer of mild-washed coffee and the third-largest overall. Juan Valdez is not just a mascot — he represents a culture where coffee farming defines entire regions and communities. A "tinto" (small black coffee) is offered everywhere as a gesture of hospitality.`,tip:"Accepting a tinto when offered is a social must — refusing feels rude even if you don't want it."},{emoji:"💃",headline:"Cali is the salsa capital of the world",body:"Caleño salsa style — fast footwork, close partner work, and dramatic spins — is considered the pinnacle of salsa dancing globally. The Feria de Cali brings over a million visitors every December. In Cali, not dancing salsa is genuinely surprising.",tip:"Even attempting Caleño footwork earns enormous respect — Colombians love when foreigners try their dance form."},{emoji:"🎪",headline:"Colombia's regions might as well be different countries",body:"Colombians from Bogotá (formal, intellectual), Medellín (warm, entrepreneurial), the Caribbean coast (loud, festive), and the Amazon (indigenous traditions) have strikingly different personalities and slang. Regional identity is everything.",tip:"Always establish where in Colombia someone is from — assumptions based on one Colombian's personality will fail you with the next."},{emoji:"🌸",headline:"Medellín transformed from world's most dangerous to most innovative",body:"In the 1990s, Medellín had the world's highest murder rate. Today it's celebrated internationally for urban innovation, cable cars connecting hillside communities, and public libraries in poor neighborhoods. Paisas (Medellín locals) are fiercely proud of this transformation.",tip:"Acknowledging Medellín's transformation — not just its past — shows you see Colombia as it is, not as it was."},{emoji:"🏆",headline:'"Parcero" and Colombian warmth are real',body:`"Parcero/a" (close friend, buddy) is Colombian slang that captures the culture's genuine warmth. Colombians are known for going out of their way to help strangers, inviting people to meals, and maintaining eye contact during conversation as a sign of respect.`,tip:`Being called "parcero" by a Colombian is a mark of genuine acceptance — it's not casual slang there, it's affection.`},{emoji:"📚",headline:"García Márquez put Colombian storytelling on the world map",body:"Gabriel García Márquez, from Aracataca on the Caribbean coast, won the Nobel Prize in Literature and invented a literary style (magical realism) that changed world fiction. Colombians quote him like gospel. His hometown is a place of pilgrimage.",tip:'Mentioning García Márquez or "Cien años de soledad" opens literary conversations that Colombians find deeply flattering.'},{emoji:"🌺",headline:"Colombia produces more flowers than almost anywhere on earth",body:"Colombia supplies around 60% of US cut-flower imports. The Medellín Flower Festival (Feria de las Flores) is one of the most spectacular events in South America — its desfile de silleteros features growers each carrying a huge flower arrangement (silleta) on their back.",tip:"The Feria de las Flores is a great conversation topic — most Colombians have strong feelings about their region's festival traditions."},{emoji:"🎵",headline:"Vallenato is the music of Colombian soul",body:"Vallenato — the accordion-driven music from the Caribbean coast — is Colombia's most emotionally resonant genre. It's about love, loss, and everyday life told through stories. Carlos Vives brought it global. It's a UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage.",tip:"Knowing one vallenato song or mentioning Carlos Vives creates immediate warmth with Colombians from the coast."}];e.Castilian=[{emoji:"🍅",headline:"La Tomatina is a battle, not a festival",body:"Every August in Buñol, Valencia, over 20,000 people throw 150,000 kilograms of ripe tomatoes at each other for exactly one hour. It started in 1945 from a street fight and became one of the world's most famous festivals. The streets run red.",tip:"Spaniards love talking about regional festivals — asking about their hometown's traditions is always a good conversation opener."},{emoji:"🌞",headline:"Siesta is real and misunderstood",body:"The siesta isn't laziness — it's a rational response to scorching afternoon heat. Shops close from roughly 2-5pm, families eat lunch together, and the streets empty. Evening life compensates: dinner at 10pm, bars until dawn. Spain runs on a different clock.",tip:"Never schedule important meetings or expect shops to be open during siesta hours — Spaniards will find the confusion amusing."},{emoji:"⚽",headline:"Football is a political and cultural battleground",body:"Real Madrid vs. Barcelona is not just a sporting rivalry — it carries historical weight between Castilian centralism and Catalan identity. Spanish football conversations require navigating regional pride carefully. Everyone has a team and strong opinions.",tip:"Ask which team someone supports before commenting on football — the answer changes everything about the conversation."},{emoji:"🥘",headline:"Tapas culture is about community, not food",body:"Tapas in Spain aren't a meal type — they're a social ritual. Standing at a bar, ordering rounds of drinks and small dishes, moving from bar to bar — this is how Spaniards build and maintain friendships. The food is secondary to the gathering.",tip:"Never rush tapas. Suggesting you move to the next bar is a compliment — it means the night is going well."},{emoji:"🎨",headline:"Spain produced Picasso, Dalí, and Goya",body:"Spain's artistic heritage is staggering. The Prado in Madrid houses one of the world's greatest collections. Dalí's theater-museum in Figueres is one of Spain's most visited museums. Spanish artists shaped the entire modern art movement.",tip:"Showing genuine interest in Spanish art — especially Velázquez or Goya — earns deep respect from culturally-minded Spaniards."},{emoji:"🗣️",headline:'Castilian Spanish uses "vosotros" — nowhere else does',body:'In Spain, the second-person plural is "vosotros" (and "vuestro"). Latin American Spanish eliminated this entirely, using "ustedes" instead. Castilian also pronounces "c" (before e/i) and "z" as a "th" sound — a feature called distincion. Both mark you immediately.',tip:'Using "vosotros" in Spain shows fluency. Using it in Latin America marks you as having learned specifically Spanish Spanish.'},{emoji:"🕺",headline:"Flamenco is from Andalusia, not all of Spain",body:"Flamenco originated in the Andalusian Roma community and is deeply tied to Romani, Moorish, and Jewish musical traditions. Most Spaniards from other regions feel about as much connection to flamenco as New Yorkers feel to country music.",tip:"Never assume all Spaniards dance or love flamenco — it's a regional art form with deep cultural roots that not all Spaniards claim."},{emoji:"🏰",headline:"Spain was Muslim for 800 years",body:"Al-Andalus, the Islamic civilization that ruled most of Spain from 711 to 1492, left permanent marks on architecture (the Alhambra), language (hundreds of Spanish words), food, and philosophy. Córdoba was once the largest city in Europe.",tip:"Spain's Moorish heritage is a fascinating topic — the Alhambra alone is worth an entire conversation with any Spaniard."},{emoji:"🥘",headline:"The midday meal is the main meal in Spain",body:"La comida (lunch, 2-4pm) is the largest and most social meal of the day. Dinner is lighter and eaten late — rarely before 9pm. Restaurants are closed between roughly 4-8pm when locals rest (la siesta).",tip:"If you're hungry at 6pm, you're eating at the tourist hour — locals won't be eating for another three hours."},{emoji:"👋",headline:"Two kisses on the cheek — always",body:"In Spain, the standard greeting between women and between men and women is dos besos — left cheek first, then right. It's done on first meetings, not just with friends. Men shake hands with other men unless close friends.",tip:"Extend your cheek first and lean left — it avoids the awkward collision that happens when one person goes right."},{emoji:"🕐",headline:"Spain runs on the wrong time zone",body:"Spain officially uses Central European Time, despite being geographically in the same zone as the UK and Portugal. This means the sun sets very late in summer (10pm in Madrid) — explaining why dinner at 10pm feels natural.",tip:"Everything in Spain runs later than you expect — adjust your clock and your expectations."}];e.Parisian=[{emoji:"🥖",headline:"The baguette is protected by French law",body:"A traditional baguette must be made on the premises where it's sold, contain only flour, water, salt, and yeast, and weigh exactly 250-300 grams. France has a national baguette competition. The morning baguette run is a daily ritual for millions of Parisians.",tip:"Complimenting the bread at a French meal is always appropriate and appreciated — it shows you understand what matters."},{emoji:"💋",headline:"La bise (cheek kiss greeting) has strict rules",body:"The number of cheek kisses varies by region: one in Brittany, two in Paris, three in Provence, four in some areas. Getting it wrong is mildly embarrassing but very French. During COVID, handshakes briefly replaced la bise and Parisians found it genuinely upsetting.",tip:"When in doubt, follow the other person's lead — initiating the wrong number of kisses is less rude than refusing la bise entirely."},{emoji:"🍷",headline:"Wine is food, not alcohol",body:"In France, wine accompanies meals as a matter of course, not as a special occasion. There are rules: white wine with fish, red with meat, rosé in summer. Ordering wine by region rather than grape variety marks you as knowledgeable. A meal without wine is considered incomplete.",tip:"Never apologize for ordering wine with lunch in France — it's normal and expected, not indulgent."},{emoji:"🗣️",headline:"Parisians appreciate attempts at French more than perfection",body:`The reputation for Parisian rudeness about language is partly earned and partly myth. What Parisians truly dislike is when tourists don't try. Starting with "Bonjour, parlez-vous anglais?" is always better than diving straight into English.`,tip:'Always greet with "Bonjour" before anything else — skipping it reads as rude, not efficient.'},{emoji:"🎨",headline:"Paris was the center of modern art for a century",body:"From Impressionism through Cubism and Surrealism, Paris attracted artists from around the world. Picasso, Modigliani, Hemingway, James Baldwin — all lived and worked here. The Louvre, Musée d'Orsay, and Centre Pompidou hold treasures that shaped how humans see.",tip:'Showing genuine interest in a specific painting or artist at a Paris museum earns more respect than general enthusiasm for "art."'},{emoji:"🧀",headline:"France has over 1,000 types of cheese",body:"De Gaulle famously asked how you govern a country with 246 varieties of cheese. The actual number is closer to 1,000. Each region has its own cheeses and feels strongly about them. A proper cheese course at dinner is a cultural institution.",tip:"Asking which regional cheese someone recommends is an excellent French conversation starter — opinions will be strong and detailed."},{emoji:"📚",headline:"Intellectual debate is a French sport",body:"Disagreeing openly, arguing positions vigorously, and changing your mind based on logic are signs of intellectual engagement in France — not rudeness. The café as a place of philosophical debate is not a cliché; it's a living tradition.",tip:"Don't be afraid to push back on a French person's argument — they'll respect you more for engaging than for agreeing politely."},{emoji:"🌹",headline:"French romanticize ordinariness",body:`The French concept of "joie de vivre" is not about grand gestures — it's about a good baguette, afternoon light through café windows, a well-made espresso. Parisians find beauty in the quotidian. Rushing past these moments is considered a failure of sensibility.`,tip:"Slowing down and noticing small pleasures — and commenting on them — is deeply French and will endear you to Parisians."}];e["Southern French"]=[{emoji:"🌿",headline:"Provence invented the outdoor market",body:"The markets of Provence — Aix-en-Provence, Arles, Apt — are not tourist attractions. They are living community institutions where locals buy food, socialize, and maintain neighborhood bonds every week. Produce, lavender, olives, and goat cheese define the region.",tip:"Shopping at a Provençal market and asking vendors about their products earns warm, extended conversations."},{emoji:"☀️",headline:"The South lives at a different pace from Paris",body:"The pace of southern France is deliberately slower — long lunches, afternoon rest, evening walks (la promenade). Parisians vacation here precisely for this quality. Locals consider the northern rush to be a kind of madness. Time is abundant here.",tip:"Never hurry anyone in the south of France — it signals that you don't understand what makes the region worth being in."},{emoji:"🥗",headline:"Bouillabaisse is Marseille's culinary identity",body:"Bouillabaisse — the saffron-scented fish stew from Marseille — has a charter specifying exactly which fish must be included and the precise order of serving. Fake versions exist everywhere; locals will tell you immediately where to find the real thing.",tip:"Asking a Marseillais where to find the best bouillabaisse is guaranteed to produce an enthusiastic, opinionated response."},{emoji:"🎭",headline:"Occitan is the soul of the south",body:'Occitan — the medieval language of troubadours and courtly love — was the dominant language of southern France for centuries. Though French replaced it, Occitan words, names, and phrases still permeate southern speech. "Adieu" as a casual goodbye is Occitan.',tip:'Knowing that "adieu" in the south means "hello" or "goodbye" casually — not the formal French farewell — prevents awkward moments.'},{emoji:"🏖️",headline:"The Côte d'Azur changed how the world vacations",body:"Before the 19th century, the Mediterranean coast was a winter destination — summer was considered too hot. English aristocrats discovered it first, then the Russian nobility, then Hollywood. The Cannes Film Festival is the direct result of this history.",tip:"The Riviera has a complex relationship with its celebrity reputation — locals often prefer conversations about the region's history over its glamour."},{emoji:"🫒",headline:"Olive oil is the Mediterranean's liquid gold",body:"The olive groves of Provence, Languedoc, and the Var have been cultivated for 2,500 years. Different varieties produce dramatically different flavors. A good Provençal olive oil is drunk, not just used for cooking. Tasting it like wine is entirely appropriate.",tip:"Showing interest in the specific variety and origin of olive oil at a southern French table will be met with genuine enthusiasm."},{emoji:"🎵",headline:"Marseille is France's most musically vibrant city",body:"Marseille's position as France's oldest city and biggest port created a musical melting pot. North African rai, Caribbean zouk, and French rap all meet here. IAM and Jul — two of France's greatest rap acts — are from Marseille.",tip:"Marseille's musical identity is a source of pride that separates it culturally from Paris — acknowledging this is always appreciated."},{emoji:"⚽",headline:"Olympique de Marseille is a religion in the south",body:"OM (Olympique de Marseille) vs. Paris Saint-Germain is France's most charged rivalry — class against glamour, south against north, local identity against billionaire investment. In Marseille, declaring your club allegiance is one of the first things people want to know.",tip:"In Marseille, saying anything complimentary about PSG is a social risk — read the room carefully."},{emoji:"🌅",headline:"The light in Provence inspired the Impressionists",body:"Cézanne painted Mont Sainte-Victoire over 60 times trying to capture the quality of Provençal light. Van Gogh moved to Arles for it. The particular clarity and warmth of Mediterranean light is not a cliché — it is genuinely different and painters noticed.",tip:"Mentioning Cézanne's connection to Aix-en-Provence earns you cultural credibility with locals who are proud of his legacy."}];e.Neapolitan=[{emoji:"🍕",headline:"Naples invented pizza and takes it personally",body:'The craft of the Neapolitan pizzaiolo holds UNESCO intangible-heritage status. The pizza itself is protected by EU "STG" rules and the Associazione Vera Pizza Napoletana, which certifies pizzerias worldwide: specific flour, San Marzano tomatoes, mozzarella, and a wood-fired oven around 430-485°C for roughly 60-90 seconds. A Neapolitan will tell you if yours is wrong.',tip:"Never eat pizza with a knife and fork in Naples — use your hands. And never put pineapple on pizza in any Italian company."},{emoji:"🎭",headline:"Pulcinella is Naples' ancient alter ego",body:"Pulcinella — the hunchbacked, masked comic character — has represented Neapolitan identity for 400 years. He is cynical, irreverent, clever despite appearing foolish, and survives everything through wit. Neapolitans see themselves in him.",tip:"Understanding Pulcinella gives insight into Neapolitan humor — dark, self-deprecating, and always finding the absurdity in hardship."},{emoji:"☕",headline:"Neapolitan coffee is in a class of its own",body:`Neapolitans believe their espresso is superior to all others and they're not wrong — the combination of water mineral content, roasting tradition, and barista technique creates something distinct. The concept of paying for a stranger's coffee ("caffè sospeso") originated here.`,tip:"Ordering a caffè sospeso (a suspended coffee paid forward for someone who can't afford one) is the most Neapolitan thing you can do."},{emoji:"🎵",headline:"Neapolitan song shaped Italian music history",body:`"O Sole Mio," "Funiculì, Funiculà," "Santa Lucia" — some of the most recognized Italian songs in history are Neapolitan songs, written in the Neapolitan dialect. Opera in Naples (San Carlo is one of the world's oldest opera houses) has a 300-year tradition.`,tip:'Knowing that "O Sole Mio" is a Neapolitan dialect song — not standard Italian — earns genuine appreciation from locals.'},{emoji:"🌋",headline:"Vesuvius is always present in Neapolitan consciousness",body:"Three million people live within range of Vesuvius — the world's most densely populated volcanic zone. Neapolitans live with this awareness daily. The volcano shaped the region's philosophy: live fully now, celebrate today, worry about tomorrow when it comes.",tip:`The philosophy of "vivere alla giornata" (living day by day) makes sense once you understand that Naples has always lived in Vesuvius' shadow.`},{emoji:"🤌",headline:"Neapolitan dialect is not just an accent",body:`Neapolitan (Napoletano) is a distinct Romance language with its own grammar, vocabulary, and literary tradition separate from standard Italian. "Guagliò" (boy/friend), "uagliò," and "jamme" (let's go) are not Italian words — they're Neapolitan.`,tip:"Using even one Neapolitan word correctly earns disproportionate warmth — Neapolitans are proud of their linguistic identity."},{emoji:"🏛️",headline:"Naples contains 2,800 years of layered history",body:"Founded by Greek colonists, conquered by Romans, ruled by Normans, Aragonese, Bourbons, and Napoleonic French — Naples absorbed every conqueror and turned them Neapolitan. The underground city (Napoli Sotterranea) contains Greek aqueducts, WWII shelters, and Roman ruins.",tip:"Showing genuine curiosity about Naples's layered history rather than just pizza and the waterfront marks you as a serious visitor."},{emoji:"🎪",headline:"Chaos in Naples is organized and purposeful",body:"Naples' famous traffic, noise, and apparent disorder follow internal logic that locals navigate effortlessly. The scooters weaving through crowds, vendors calling out, impromptu conversations blocking sidewalks — this is community in motion, not breakdown.",tip:"Never express frustration with Neapolitan chaos out loud — locals will interpret it as not understanding their city's energy."}];e.Sicilian=[{emoji:"🍊",headline:"Sicily feeds Europe",body:"Sicily produces vast quantities of citrus, olives, almonds, pistachios, and tomatoes for the entire continent. The blood orange — with its deep red flesh and unique flavor — exists because of Sicily's specific combination of volcanic soil and temperature swings. Food here is exceptionally serious.",tip:"Asking about local seasonal produce and where to find it earns rich, detailed responses from Sicilians who take agricultural pride seriously."},{emoji:"🏺",headline:"Sicily was the center of the ancient Mediterranean world",body:"The Greek temples of Agrigento (Valle dei Templi) are among the best-preserved in the world — better than most in Greece. Syracuse was once the largest Greek city outside Greece. Sicily was fought over by every Mediterranean civilization because it was the most valuable island on Earth.",tip:"Showing knowledge of Sicily's Greek and Arab heritage — not just Norman or Italian — demonstrates cultural depth that Sicilians appreciate."},{emoji:"🎵",headline:"Sicilian music carries millennia of Mediterranean influence",body:"Sicilian folk music (canti siciliani) carries Arabic microtonal inflections, Greek modal scales, and Spanish rhythms layered over centuries. The marranzano (jew's harp) and the friscalettu (cane flute) give it a sound genuinely different from mainland Italian music.",tip:"Asking about traditional Sicilian music rather than Italian pop opens completely different and deeper conversations."},{emoji:"🧁",headline:"Sicilian pastry is among the world's greatest",body:"Cannoli, cassata, granita, arancini — Sicilian pastry and street food were shaped by Arab influence during the 9th-11th century occupation. Marzipan shaped into fruit (frutta di Martorana) dates to 11th-century convents. The granita here is nothing like what you've had elsewhere.",tip:"Ordering granita con brioche for breakfast like a local — not as a dessert — immediately marks you as culturally informed."},{emoji:"🌊",headline:"Sicilians have a complex relationship with the mainland",body:"Sicily was officially united with Italy only in 1860 and some Sicilians still feel more Sicilian than Italian. There's a proud separateness — a sense that Sicilian culture, language, and history are distinct from the Italian mainland's. This is not resentment; it's identity.",tip:"Treating Sicily as distinct from Italy — asking about specifically Sicilian culture — is always received better than lumping it in with the mainland."},{emoji:"🤝",headline:"Sicilian hospitality is an obligation and an art",body:"In Sicily, failing to offer food and drink to a guest is considered a serious lapse. Hospitality (accoglienza) is not optional — it's structural. Guests are treated with formality and abundance regardless of the household's means. Refusing offerings is rude.",tip:"Always accept what's offered in a Sicilian home — multiple times if necessary. The host will offer again to be sure you're satisfied."},{emoji:"🌋",headline:"Etna is active and central to Sicilian identity",body:"Mount Etna is the highest active volcano in Europe and has shaped Sicilian agriculture, geology, and consciousness for millennia. The fertile volcanic soil produces world-class wine and food. Etna wine (from the volcano's slopes) is among Italy's most exciting emerging appellations.",tip:"Showing knowledge of Etna wines and their specific characteristics earns enormous respect from food-focused Sicilians."},{emoji:"🗣️",headline:"Sicilian dialect contains Arabic, Greek, and Norman words",body:'Sicilian (Sicilianu) is a separate Romance language with vocabulary drawn from Arabic ("giarra" from Arabic jarrah), Greek ("strattu" from Greek), and Norman French. It was the first literary language of the Italian peninsula — Dante studied Sicilian poets.',tip:"Knowing that Sicilian dialect is linguistically distinct from Italian — not just an accent — is a mark of respect that Sicilians genuinely appreciate."}];e.Haiti=[{emoji:"🌟",headline:"Haiti led the only successful slave revolution in history",body:"In 1804, Haiti became the first Black republic in the world after enslaved Africans defeated Napoleon's army — the greatest military power of the era. This is considered one of the most significant events in human history. Haitian independence inspired liberation movements worldwide.",tip:"Acknowledging Haiti's revolutionary history — with genuine respect — is the single most powerful cultural connection you can make with Haitians."},{emoji:"🎨",headline:"Haitian art is among the most vibrant in the world",body:"Haitian naive art — vivid, detailed paintings of daily life, Vodou ceremonies, and historical events — is internationally collected and exhibited in major museums. The Centre d'Art in Port-au-Prince launched a global movement in the 1940s. Haitian art is an industry and a cultural identity.",tip:"Showing knowledge of Haitian painting tradition — even just recognizing the style — earns genuine surprise and appreciation from Haitians."},{emoji:"🥁",headline:"Vodou is a religion, not a horror movie",body:"Vodou (Haitian Voodoo) is a West African-derived spiritual tradition blending Fon, Ewe, and Yoruba religion with Catholicism. It has a complex theology, ethical system, and community function. Hollywood's zombie mythology has nothing to do with actual Vodou practice.",tip:"Approaching Vodou with genuine curiosity rather than horror-movie assumptions earns deep respect from Haitians who are tired of the misrepresentation."},{emoji:"🎵",headline:"Kompa is Haiti's national rhythm",body:"Kompa (compas) — a slow, sensual dance music developed in the 1950s — is Haiti's dominant popular music. Nemours Jean-Baptiste created it by blending merengue with jazz. You'll hear it at every party, wedding, and celebration. Learning to dance kompa earns immediate social currency.",tip:"Attempting to dance kompa — even badly — is received as an act of love toward Haitian culture."},{emoji:"🌺",headline:"Haitian Creole is the language of identity and heart",body:"While French is Haiti's official language, Haitian Creole is what 100% of Haitians speak at home, with friends, and from the heart. It developed on the plantations as a communication language between enslaved Africans of different origins and French colonizers. It is nobody's broken French.",tip:"Never call Creole a dialect of French or broken French — it is a complete, distinct language with its own grammar and history."},{emoji:"🌶️",headline:"Haitian food is boldly spiced and deeply communal",body:"Griot (fried pork), diri ak djon djon (black mushroom rice), tassot (dried beef), and pikliz (spicy pickled vegetables) define Haitian cuisine. Epis — a base of garlic, peppers, herbs, and citrus — flavors everything. Meals are events, not just sustenance.",tip:"Asking about epis and how someone makes theirs opens an extended, passionate culinary conversation — every family has their version."},{emoji:"💪",headline:'"Nou pap janm kase" — we will never break',body:`Haitian resilience in the face of catastrophe — earthquakes, hurricanes, political instability, poverty — is not passive endurance. It is active, creative, and proud. The phrase "Nou pap janm kase" (we will never break) is not a slogan; it's lived experience.`,tip:"Acknowledging Haitian resilience specifically — not pity — is the respectful framing that Haitians respond to positively."},{emoji:"🏔️",headline:'Haiti means "land of high mountains" in Taíno',body:'The Taíno people, indigenous to Hispaniola, named their island "Ayiti" — land of high mountains. When the revolution succeeded in 1804, the new nation chose this indigenous name rather than any colonial one. It was an act of reclamation and pride.',tip:'Knowing the etymology of "Haiti" and its connection to Taíno heritage shows the kind of historical depth that Haitians find meaningful.'},{emoji:"🍲",headline:"Joumou soup is eaten on January 1st to celebrate freedom",body:"Soup joumou (pumpkin soup) was the food forbidden to enslaved Haitians under French colonial rule. On January 1, 1804, when independence was declared, Haitians ate it publicly for the first time. Now it's prepared every New Year's Day as a direct act of historical memory.",tip:"Understanding soup joumou as a political and emotional tradition — not just food — shows deep cultural respect."},{emoji:"🤝",headline:"Collective community support (Konbit) is built into Haitian culture",body:"Konbit is the traditional Haitian practice of collective labor — neighbors helping each other with harvests, construction, or community projects. It's a pre-colonial African tradition that survived enslavement and shapes how communities support each other.",tip:"Understanding konbit explains why Haitian community response to crisis is often faster and more organized than outside observers expect."}];e["Quebec French"]=[...e["Quebec French"]||[],{emoji:"❄️",headline:"Quebec's winter is a source of identity, not just weather",body:"With temperatures reaching -30°C and several meters of snow annually, Québécois have built an entire culture around winter. The Quebec Winter Carnival (Carnaval de Québec) is the largest winter festival in the world. The Bonhomme Carnaval is a national icon.",tip:"Complaining about Quebec winter to a Québécois is dangerous — they'll either mock you gently or lecture you about resilience."},{emoji:"🗣️",headline:"Joual is working-class Montreal French with deep roots",body:`Joual — the working-class French dialect of Montreal — was dismissed as "bad French" for centuries. In the 1960s, Québécois playwrights and poets reclaimed it as an authentic voice. Michel Tremblay's plays in joual were revolutionary. It's not broken French — it's a social identity.`,tip:"Never correct a Québécois's French by pointing to Parisian standards — the comparison is politically and culturally loaded."},{emoji:"🍁",headline:"The Quiet Revolution changed everything",body:"In the 1960s, Quebec transformed from a conservative, church-dominated society to a secular, progressive one in a single decade. The Catholic Church lost its grip. The state took over education and healthcare. Québécois became proud nationalists. This period is called La Révolution Tranquille.",tip:"Understanding the Quiet Revolution explains why Quebec is simultaneously very French and very North American — it created a unique hybrid identity."},{emoji:"🎵",headline:"Quebec music is confidently distinct from French music",body:"Céline Dion is Quebec's most famous export, but the local music scene — with artists like Coeur de Pirate, Les Cowboys Fringants, and Ariane Moffatt — is deeply tied to Quebec identity. Québécois music explores themes of language, land, and belonging in ways that Paris never would.",tip:"Asking about Quebec music rather than French music signals that you understand Quebec as culturally distinct — which Québécois deeply appreciate."},{emoji:"🍺",headline:"Poutine is sacred and serious",body:"Poutine — french fries, cheese curds, and gravy — originated in rural Quebec in the late 1950s. It's now a national symbol. But Québécois are serious about the specifics: the curds must squeak when fresh, the gravy must be exactly right. Every region has its version.",tip:"Never order poutine with shredded cheese instead of fresh curds in Quebec — locals will consider it a moral failure."},{emoji:"🏒",headline:"Hockey is Quebec's religion, the Canadiens its church",body:`The Montreal Canadiens (Les Habitants, or "le CH") have won 24 Stanley Cup championships — the most in NHL history. For Québécois, hockey is culture, language, and community wrapped into one sport. Rocket Richard's 1955 riot shows how deeply the team is embedded in Quebec identity.`,tip:"Knowing the Canadiens' history — at least the name Rocket Richard — earns immediate credibility with Québécois hockey fans."},{emoji:"🌊",headline:"The St. Lawrence River shaped Quebec civilization",body:"The St. Lawrence — which Cartier first navigated in 1534 — is the reason Quebec exists where it does. It was the highway for the fur trade, the reason for Quebec City's fortress position, and still defines the province's geography, economy, and self-understanding.",tip:"References to the fleuve (river) rather than rivière (the St. Lawrence is specifically called le fleuve) signal linguistic fluency that impresses Québécois."}];export{u as a,c as b,h as g};
